# MCSP Qualification Residue Reduction 0.17

Status: Constructive reduction
Qualification: Under Conditional Experiment
Parent: Determinate Condition Kernel 0.16

## Objective

Reduce the remaining residue:

    evidence
    incompatibility
    interpretation boundary
    support
    exclusion

Do not promote qualification vocabulary into ontology unless necessary.

## Evidence

EVIDENCE is not currently required as an MCSP ontological primitive.

It is a qualification role: a determinate condition or qualified relation may be used when testing a semantic claim.

Therefore:

    evidence := role in qualification procedure

not:

    EVIDENCE := fundamental kind of thing

Status: PROCEDURAL / DERIVED.

## Support

SUPPORT likewise describes a qualification relation between determination and a claimed interpretation.

It does not require independent ontology.

Provisional:

    SUPPORT(K,I,B)
      iff I remains admissible under the determinations of K within boundary B.

Status: DERIVED QUALIFICATION RELATION.

## Exclusion

EXCLUSION need not be primitive.

An interpretation is excluded when it fails admissibility under the same determination and boundary used for the qualification test.

    EXCLUDE(K,I,B)
      := NOT ADMIT(K,I,B)

The NOT notation is documentary and does not establish Boolean kernel semantics.

Status: DERIVED QUALIFICATION RESULT.

## Incompatibility

Two interpretations are incompatible when they cannot both remain admissible under the same relevant determination and interpretation boundary while preserving their asserted semantic invariants.

    INCOMPATIBLE(I,J,K,B)
      := no joint admissibility under K and B

This is relational qualification, not a new ontological object.

Status: DERIVED.

## Interpretation boundary

BOUNDARY survives the reduction.

Without a boundary, competing interpretations can silently change:
- observable distinctions;
- relevant relations;
- scope;
- constraints;
- realization assumptions.

Then qualification becomes meaningless because the comparison does not hold anything invariant.

The boundary does not have to be spatial.

It is the declared limit of what participates in an interpretation/qualification.

Status: IRREDUCIBLE ROLE CANDIDATE.

## Boundary versus SCOPE

Earlier MCSP retained SCOPE as an irreducible semantic role.

Interpretation boundary may be the pre-semantic ancestor of SCOPE.

Hypothesis:

    BOUNDARY
      -> interpreted SCOPE

If true, SCOPE is not kernel-level but a semantic interpretation of boundary.

This requires further attack because "limit" and "participates" remain residue.

## Revised qualification rule

For determinate condition K, interpretation I, boundary B:

    QUALIFY(K,I,B)

requires:
1. B is held invariant for the comparison;
2. K provides determination relevant within B;
3. at least one materially incompatible interpretation is not jointly admissible under the same K and B;
4. I does not contradict the invoked canonical invariants;
5. host realization details do not silently redefine B or I.

No Boolean truth ontology is implied.

## Kernel consequence

The kernel candidate is now:

    DETERMINATE CONDITION
        within an INTERPRETATION BOUNDARY

But BOUNDARY is not yet admitted into the kernel.

It may instead be required only at the first act of interpretation.

Thus distinguish:

    pre-interpretation:
        determinate condition

    interpretation qualification:
        determinate condition + boundary

This keeps the kernel smaller.

## Driver consequence

A driver may declare only the boundary relevant to the semantic contract it exposes.

Unknown or irrelevant device conditions may remain outside that boundary.

Therefore qualification can be local rather than requiring total device ontology.

## Canonical consequence

A canonical semantic graph defines invariants.

Application to a realization is qualified only within a declared boundary.

Two realizations may differ outside the boundary and still preserve the same canonical semantics.

This is necessary for host independence.

## Tests to break 0.17

R1:
Can qualification work with no invariant boundary at all?

R2:
Can two interpretations be compared if each chooses its own observables?

R3:
Can boundary be reduced entirely to determination without losing comparison scope?

R4:
Can boundary be reduced to relation without presupposing which relations participate?

R5:
Can SCOPE be derived from interpreted boundary?

R6:
Can a driver qualify a semantic subset while leaving other conditions uninterpreted?

R7:
Can NASM/GAS realizations differ outside B while projecting the same canonical within B?

## Current result

Reduced from ontology:
    evidence
    support
    exclusion
    incompatibility

Survivor:
    interpretation boundary

Current kernel hypothesis remains:
    DETERMINATE CONDITION

Current first-interpretation requirement:
    BOUNDARY

Next:
Attack BOUNDARY and determine whether it is intrinsic to condition, intrinsic to interpretation, or derivable from determination.
